    'body' => <<<'HTML'
<p>In many gardens the fence is treated as an afterthought: a line that marks where your plot stops and the neighbour's begins. In a small garden it carries far more weight. It is often the largest surface you can see from the house, and it decides whether the space feels like a tight box or a calm, contemporary outdoor room. Horizontal slat fences are popular for exactly this reason. Long, level lines lead the eye along the boundary rather than up it, which gives a compact plot a sense of breadth and continuity that upright boards rarely achieve.</p>
<p>Getting it right in a small space is a balance of privacy, light and proportion. Below are eight horizontal slat fence ideas for small yards and gardens, followed by the practical details that matter most at close range: slat sizes, gaps, post spacing, planning rules and child safety.</p>

<h2 id="natural-cedar">1. Natural cedar and other durable softwoods</h2>
<p>Cedar is one of the most popular timbers for horizontal fencing. It has good natural resistance to decay, stays fairly stable as it wets and dries, and its colour varies from pale honey to deep reddish brown, so a run of plain slats still has texture. In a small garden that warmth softens the boundary without dominating it.</p>
<p>In the UK, larch and thermally modified timber are good alternatives with similar character. Whichever you choose, think about the finish:</p>
<ul>
<li><strong>Clear or lightly tinted oil</strong> keeps the timber pale, which reflects more light into a shaded plot than a dark stain.</li>
<li><strong>Leaving it untreated</strong> lets cedar and larch weather to a soft silver-grey. Many people like this, but it is uneven at first, especially where one part of the fence gets more rain or sun.</li>
<li><strong>Kiln-dried, straight-grained boards</strong> are worth asking for. A twisted or cupped board is much easier to spot in a horizontal run than in a vertical one.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="black-composite">2. Black or charcoal composite</h2>
<p>A dark fence in a small garden sounds risky, but black and charcoal tend to recede. The boundary seems to sit further back, and green leaves, pale stems and flowers stand out sharply against it. The effect is similar to painting the back wall of a small room a deep colour.</p>
<p>Composite slats are low maintenance rather than no maintenance: there is no restaining, but they still need washing occasionally to remove dirt, pollen and algae, following the manufacturer's care guidance. Their even colour and crisp edges suit townhouses and modern extensions. Check two things before you buy. First, composite boards are heavy and can be less stiff than timber, so they usually need the manufacturer's own posts or closer post spacing. Second, dark surfaces get hot in strong sun, which matters if you plan to sit or grow plants right up against them.</p>

<h2 id="varied-widths">3. Varied slat widths</h2>
<p>If a run of identical slats looks flat, mix the widths. A repeating pattern of narrow and wide boards, for example 45 mm, 70 mm and 95 mm (roughly 2, 3 and 4 in), gives the fence a rhythm that keeps the eye moving along it. The boundary then reads as a designed feature rather than a barrier that sits a little too close.</p>
<p>A few rules keep it looking deliberate rather than random:</p>
<ul>
<li>Use no more than three widths, and repeat the sequence so it has a clear pattern.</li>
<li>Keep the gaps the same even when the widths change; inconsistent gaps look like a mistake.</li>
<li>Put the widest boards near the bottom and top for a solid frame, with narrower slats in the middle.</li>
<li>Lay the sequence out on the ground first and step back to check it from the house.</li>
</ul>
<p>In a pocket-sized garden this kind of detail can do the job of a piece of outdoor art, so you need fewer ornaments competing for space.</p>

<h2 id="open-gaps">4. Open gaps for light and air</h2>
<p>A completely solid fence can feel heavy in a very small garden. Opening up the gaps between slats lets light, air and glimpses of the world beyond pass through, so the space feels screened rather than shut in.</p>
<p>Gaps of around 5–25 mm (¼–1 in) cover most designs. At the lower end you get near-privacy from most angles; at the upper end the fence becomes a light, breezy screen that throws striped shadows across paving and planting. Tight gaps for privacy still need a small expansion allowance, as recommended by your timber supplier, because boards swell in wet weather. Gaps also reduce how much wind the fence catches, which helps plants along the boundary and takes some strain off the posts in exposed spots.</p>

<h2 id="planted-panels">5. Slatted panels as a planting frame</h2>
<p>A horizontal slat fence makes a natural framework for wall-mounted planting. The level boards and gaps give you easy fixing points for slim planters, hooks and a drip line, so you can grow herbs, trailing plants or small flowering perennials without giving up any patio or lawn.</p>
<p>Before you fill it with compost, protect both the fence and the planting:</p>
<ul>
<li>Check the fence and posts can take the extra weight once planters are wet, and spread the load across several boards and posts.</li>
<li>Use spacers or brackets to hold planters slightly off the timber, so air can circulate and damp does not sit against the boards.</li>
<li>Choose planters with drainage that runs away from the fence, not down its face.</li>
<li>Make sure the fence is yours; if it belongs to a neighbour, ask before fixing anything to it.</li>
</ul>

<h2 id="integrated-lighting">6. Integrated lighting</h2>
<p>Lighting does more work in a small garden than almost anything else, and a slatted fence can become part of the scheme. Low-level LED strip tucked under the lip of one or two slats, or small recessed lights set into a thicker capping board, can wash a soft glow across the timber. Because the slats run horizontally, light grazing along them picks out the grain and shadow lines beautifully.</p>
<p>Use fittings that are rated for outdoor use, run cables where they will not be damaged, and have mains-powered lighting installed by a qualified electrician. Low-voltage and solar options are easier to add later, but still follow the manufacturer's instructions on mounting and weather protection.</p>

<h2 id="metal-posts">7. Metal posts with timber slats</h2>
<p>Pairing timber slats with slim metal posts, usually black powder-coated aluminium or steel, gives a framed, architectural look. The posts are visually lighter than chunky 100 × 100 mm (4 × 4 in) timber posts and they do not rot at ground level, while the slats keep the warmth of wood.</p>
<p>This combination suits gardens with metal furniture, black window frames or other modern details. Many systems use a channel in the post that the slats slide into, which keeps the lines very straight. Follow the system's guidance on post depth, footings and maximum slat length, and use stainless or coated fixings so they do not stain the timber.</p>

<h2 id="shadow-box">8. Shadow box</h2>
<p>A shadow box fence has slats fixed alternately to the front and back of the posts or rails, with each board overlapping the gaps on the opposite side. Seen from an angle it looks solid; seen straight on it has depth, and the shadows shift as the sun moves round. You get strong privacy with air still passing through.</p>
<p>It also looks the same from both sides, which is a real advantage when a neighbour shares the view. The trade-offs are that it uses more boards than a single-sided fence and is thicker, so it takes a little more ground along a narrow boundary.</p>

<h2 id="technical-considerations">Getting the details right in a small garden</h2>
<p>You stand much closer to the fence in a small garden, so small errors are obvious. These points make the difference between a crisp result and one that looks slightly off.</p>
<h3>Level, alignment and fixings</h3>
<p>Horizontal lines that drift even slightly are easy to spot. Set a string line or laser level for the first board, check every few boards, and keep screw heads in neat vertical lines. Use stainless steel or coated screws suited to the timber, as some species, cedar included, can stain around plain steel.</p>
<h3>Slat size and proportion</h3>
<p>Common slat widths are around 45–95 mm (2–4 in). In very small spaces, narrower boards usually look finer and less heavy; wide boards can feel bulky at close range. Thicker boards resist sagging better over longer spans.</p>
<h3>Post spacing</h3>
<p>Because the boards span between posts, a horizontal fence usually needs posts closer than a vertical board fence, often around 1.2–1.8 m (4–6 ft) depending on board thickness. On longer runs, add a mid-post or a vertical stiffener fixed to the back of the slats to stop them bowing over time.</p>
<h3>Planning rules</h3>
<p>Rules vary. In England, fences are generally permitted development up to 2 m (6 ft 6 in) high, or 1 m (3 ft 3 in) next to a highway used by vehicles, but listed buildings, conservation areas and some new-build estates have extra restrictions. In the US, height and setback limits are set locally and by HOAs. Check with your local planning authority before you build, and agree boundary positions with neighbours.</p>
<h3>Child safety</h3>
<p>Horizontal slats can work like a ladder for children. Avoid them as the barrier around a pool, pond or a raised drop, and check local pool-barrier rules, which often restrict climbable horizontal rails on the outside face. Also avoid gaps that could trap a small child's head or limbs. Where young children play, keep gaps narrow and consider vertical boards or a smooth panel for that stretch of boundary.</p>

<blockquote><p><strong>Tip:</strong> Before ordering, screw two or three sample slats to a scrap frame with your chosen gaps and lean it against the boundary. Look at it from the back door and from your seating area at different times of day; it is the quickest way to judge width, gap and colour in your own light.</p></blockquote>

<h2 id="faq">FAQ</h2>
<h3>Does a horizontal fence make a yard look smaller or larger?</h3>
<p>Generally larger, or at least wider. The long lines carry the eye along the boundary, which makes a narrow plot read broader. In a very short, shallow garden, combine it with some taller planting so the space does not feel squashed.</p>
<h3>Is a horizontal slat fence more expensive than a vertical one?</h3>
<p>Usually, yes. Horizontal designs need straighter, better-quality boards to avoid visible sagging and twisting, more posts or stiffeners because of the shorter spans, and more careful installation to keep every line level. Wider gaps and simpler finishes can help keep it reasonable.</p>
<h3>How do I stop the slats sagging over time?</h3>
<p>Keep the span between posts short, around 1.2–1.8 m (4–6 ft) depending on board thickness, use kiln-dried boards of a sensible thickness, and add a mid-post or vertical stiffener on longer runs. Fix each board at every post, not just at the ends.</p>
<h3>What is the best wood for a horizontal fence?</h3>
<p>Cedar, larch and thermally modified timber are good choices in the UK; in the US, cedar and redwood are common, and ipe is used for a hardwood finish. If you choose a tropical hardwood, look for FSC or PEFC certification. Pressure-treated pine is cheaper but more prone to warping and twisting in a horizontal layout.</p>
<h3>How much space should there be between the slats?</h3>
<p>Around 5–25 mm (¼–1 in) covers most designs: tighter for privacy, wider for a light, open screen. Even tight gaps need a small expansion allowance per your timber supplier. If children or pets use the garden, avoid gaps that could trap a head, paw or limb.</p>

<h2 id="final-thoughts">Final thoughts</h2>
<p>A small garden does not limit your design options; it sharpens them. A well-detailed horizontal slat fence brings light, rhythm and a sense of width to a compact plot, whether you choose warm cedar, dark composite or timber framed by slim metal posts. For more screening options, see our <a href="/blog/privacy-fence-small-yard-ideas">privacy fence ideas for small yards</a>, and if you plan to build it yourself, read <a href="/blog/how-to-build-a-wood-fence">how to build a wood fence</a>. To design the whole outdoor space around your new boundary, explore our <a href="/services/balcony-terrace-decoration">balcony and terrace decoration</a> service or <a href="/book-consultation">book a consultation</a>.</p>
